// Found running the complete examples aggregator under valgrind: `process.
// argv` (`emit_process_argv`) builds a fresh `string[]` via
// `tyrus_process_argv` and returned it unclaimed, no
// `record_owned_native_result` anywhere, leaking every call. Closed by
// registering the array result right after loading it out of its own
// temporary slot, the same place any other allocating native call already
// claims itself.
//
// Two sibling gaps in the same area, `process.env.SOME_VAR`
// (`ExprKind::FieldAccess` on the `process.env` namespace, calling
// `emit_env_get` and returning its fresh `tyrus_env_get_str` result with no
// registration at all) and `const { KEY } = process.env;`
// (`StmtKind::ObjectDestructure`, which shares the same runtime call but
// binds each getter's result straight into its own alloca via `store_var`,
// bypassing the ownership-claiming path a normal `VarDecl` goes through),
// were found and closed the same way (registering the field-access result,
// and `mark_owned_synchronous_value`-claiming each destructured `Str`
// binding). Neither is exercised in this file: `scripts/leak-check` builds
// each case in a throwaway project with no `.env`, and this project's env
// schema is declared entirely by a `.env` file's own `@type(...)` comments,
// a case that reads `process.env` here would fail to build for having no
// declared schema, not for anything ownership-related. Both fixes were
// confirmed clean by hand, in a scratch project with a real `.env`, before
// landing.
//
// Expected output is true.
const main = (): void => {
    const args: string[] = process.argv;
    console.log(args.length >= 1);
    console.log("assert:", true);
};
